{"name":"Row House","hex":"#bfb8ad","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"row house","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A variety of residential building where the individual houses lining a street share adjacent walls in common and have a continuous stretch of roof"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/row_house"}],"entries":[{"title":"Row House (Hallowell, Maine)","blocks":[{"text":"The Row House is a historic multiunit tenement house at 106-114 2nd Street in downtown Hallowell, Maine. Built in 1840, it is one of a small number of row houses built in 19th-century Maine, and is believed to be the oldest built of wood. It was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1970."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Rowhouse_in_Hallowell.jpg/330px-Rowhouse_in_Hallowell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Row_House_(Hallowell%2C_Maine)","wikidata":"Q25340068"},{"title":"Hill of Row","blocks":[{"text":"The Hill of Row is the high ground south east of Doune in Stirlingshire."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hill_of_Row","wikidata":"Q16984147"},{"title":"Terraced house","blocks":[{"text":"A terrace or terraced house (UK) or townhouse (US) is a type of medium-density housing which first started in 16th century Europe with a row of joined houses sharing side walls. In the United States and Canada these are sometimes known as row houses or row homes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Place_Vosges_Paris_Mai_2006_011.jpg/330px-Place_Vosges_Paris_Mai_2006_011.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terraced_house","wikidata":"Q875016"},{"title":"House","blocks":[{"text":"A house is a residential building. It may range in complexity from a rudimentary hut to a complex structure of wood, masonry, concrete or other material, outfitted with plumbing, electrical, and heating, ventilation, and air conditioning systems. Houses use a range of different roofing systems to keep precipitation such as rain from getting into the dwelling space. Houses generally have doors or locks to secure the dwelling space and protect its inhabitants and contents from burglars or other trespassers. Most conventional modern houses in Western cultures will contain one or more bedrooms and bathrooms, a kitchen or cooking area, and a living room. A house may have a separate dining room, or the eating area may be integrated into the kitchen or another room. Some large houses in North America have a recreation room. In traditional agriculture-oriented societies, domestic animals such as chickens or larger livestock may share part of the house with humans."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Katsura_Imperial_Villa_in_Spring.jpg/330px-Katsura_Imperial_Villa_in_Spring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House","wikidata":"Q3947"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/2/28/RowHouseDoune.jpg/500px-RowHouseDoune.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/2/28/RowHouseDoune.jpg/1280px-RowHouseDoune.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/2/28/RowHouseDoune.jpg/500px-RowHouseDoune.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Row House","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARowHouseDoune.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Wfm_david_stirling_statue.jpg/500px-Wfm_david_stirling_statue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Wfm_dav